---
/**
 * Brief behind the "Hiring slows" row on /economics/.
 * Title stays "Payrolls and the jobs mix" (Stage 1 / Stage 4). Backstory is
 * dated text. Where we stand and the levels in What we're watching are built
 * from the row's feeds at build time (src/lib/briefs.ts), so they move with
 * every feed push. The old digest is in docs/archive/pages/economics/.
 */
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import RiskWatch from '../../components/RiskWatch.astro';
import { hiringBriefNow } from '../../lib/briefs';
import '../../styles/economics.css';

const title = 'Payrolls and the jobs mix';
const subhead = 'Hiring people who do the work with AI, not only people who manage that work. The numbers below come from the /economics/ watch-list feed.';

const brief = hiringBriefNow();

const watchItems = [
  ...brief.watch,
  'The next Employment Situation release from the Bureau of Labor Statistics.',
  'Whether food services, local government education, and information keep driving the industry mix the August release named.',
];

const sources = [
  {
    text: 'U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, The Employment Situation, August 2026 (released 4 Sep 2026)',
    href: 'https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/empsit_09042026.htm',
  },
  {
    text: 'CNBC, August 2026 jobs report and the Dow Jones estimate, 4 Sep 2026',
    href: 'https://www.cnbc.com/2026/09/04/jobs-report-august-2026.html',
  },
  {
    text: 'Anthropic, Computer use tool documentation',
    href: 'https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/computer-use-tool',
  },
  {
    text: 'U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, The Employment Situation, September 2026 (released 2 Oct 2026)',
    href: 'https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/empsit_10022026.htm',
  },
  {
    text: 'U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, Current Employment Statistics',
    href: 'https://www.bls.gov/ces/',
  },
  {
    text: 'U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, Current Population Survey',
    href: 'https://www.bls.gov/cps/',
  },
  {
    text: 'FRED, Initial Claims (ICSA)',
    href: 'https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/ICSA',
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} - Rycode`}
  description={subhead}
  variant="economics"
>
  <article class="econ-digest econ-brief">
    <header class="econ-digest-head">
      <h1 class="econ-digest-title">{title}</h1>
      <p class="econ-digest-subhead">{subhead}</p>
    </header>

    <div class="econ-digest-main">
      <section class="econ-digest-section" id="backstory">
        <h2>Backstory</h2>
        <p>On 4 Sep 2026 the Bureau of Labor Statistics reported that nonfarm payroll employment, the count of jobs at businesses and government, rose by 162,000 in August. Unemployment held at 4.1 percent. Average hourly earnings rose 0.3 percent on the month and 3.1 percent over the year. Economists surveyed by Dow Jones had expected 53,000, according to CNBC. June and July were revised up by a combined 55,000. Food services and drinking places added 59,000 jobs and local government education added 42,000. The information industry lost jobs.</p>
        <p>The open question after that print was the mix: who gets hired next. Frontier models now offer computer use, meaning the model can operate a desktop, open apps, click, type, and run multi-step tasks. When an agent can run the computer, the scarce skills become judging what to build, catching errors, and owning the outcome. The hiring sentence is blunt: hire people who do the work with AI, not a stack of managers watching managers.</p>
        <p>On 2 Oct 2026 the September report showed payrolls up 29,000 and unemployment at 4.2 percent, so the August pace did not repeat. The product capability behind that hiring sentence is already shipping.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="econ-digest-section" id="where-we-stand">
        <h2>Where we stand</h2>
        <p class="econ-brief-asof">{brief.asOf}</p>
        <p>{brief.payrolls}</p>
        {brief.augustRevision && <p>{brief.augustRevision}</p>}
        <p>{brief.unemployment}</p>
        {brief.claims && <p>{brief.claims}</p>}
        <p>{brief.strain}</p>
      </section>

      <section class="econ-digest-section" id="watching">
        <h2>What we're watching</h2>
        <RiskWatch items={watchItems} class="econ-watch" />
      </section>

      <section class="econ-digest-section econ-digest-sources" id="sources">
        <h2>Sources</h2>
        <ol>
          {sources.map((source) => (
            <li>
              <a href={source.href}>{source.text}</a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
